{"name":"Sidecar","hex":"#e9d9a9","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sidecar","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A one-wheeled attachment to the side of a motorcycle to allow for a separate seat for a passenger or cargo space.","A cocktail made with cognac (or brandy), triple sec liqueur, and lemon juice."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sidecar"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sidecar","blocks":[{"text":"A sidecar is a one-wheeled device attached to the side of a motorcycle, scooter, or bicycle, making the whole a three-wheeled vehicle. The combination of a motorcycle with a sidecar is sometimes called a hack, outfit, rig, tricycle, or tuk-tuk."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/NSU_601_OSL_Steib.jpg/330px-NSU_601_OSL_Steib.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sidecar","wikidata":"Q1051405"},{"title":"Sidecar (cocktail)","blocks":[{"text":"The sidecar is a cocktail traditionally made with brandy, orange liqueur, and lemon juice. It became popular in Paris and London in the early 1920s. Common modifications of the original recipe are a sugar rim, added sugar syrup, and an orange twist or lemon twist."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Sidecar-cocktail.jpg/330px-Sidecar-cocktail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sidecar_(cocktail)","wikidata":"Q1134797"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-12561%2C_Berlin%2C_Fahrrad_mit_Beiwagen.jpg/500px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-12561%2C_Berlin%2C_Fahrrad_mit_Beiwagen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-12561%2C_Berlin%2C_Fahrrad_mit_Beiwagen.jpg/500px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-12561%2C_Berlin%2C_